{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/634386-help-incubus-induced-condition","id":634386,"title":"Help: incubus induced condition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-21T02:53:16-07:00","id":634386,"title":"Help: incubus induced condition","content":"Last session my group got into conflict with an incubus while invading somebody's dream, the incubus that feeds on shame (forgot its name).<br /> <br /> The 1e mechanics are simple in that the victim does not gain willpower for the night and if they truly commit a sin loose clarity for a short time. I am not an overly big fan of the mechanic and had it been intended as more than a side track I would have given it more thought. Players being players, it turned out much more then a mere distraction, generating some excellent roleplaying as well. Anyway, what penalty is a temporary reduction in clarity if it never comes up in play because the characters are able to take a rest long enough for it to go away? In 2e the condition rules though are perfect to make the actions of the characters in the dream have clear consequences that come into play. After all, a condition is removed through actions in play and not simply by waiting. The two players themselves were also asking for it, so it is not as if I am unreasonable for wanting it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The problem? What makes a good condition in this particular case? We have one PC who snapped after seeing a drug dealer mistreating his drug addicted sister and he started slowly killing the drug dealer (playing into his vice of cruelty and his guilt of abandoning his sister to his drug addicted mother when he was kidnapped). The other PC smashed up the security room they were in, including some of the 'guards' after seeing the things his fetch did on the security monitors (the fetch committed a war crime just after the PC's kidnapping) with the fetch mocking him. It played upon the PC's temper issues and his guilt on that war crime and how much it had hurt his parents and grandfather. Both failed their clarity checks, and both ended up with a temporary insanity under the old rules.<br /> <br /> Suggestions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4560-madfox11","name":"Madfox11","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T16:12:09-07:00","id":636389,"title":"","content":"Here's the Swooning condition:<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">A character may have multiple instances of this Condition, reflecting affection for multiple characters. He suffers a \u20132 die penalty to any rolls that would adversely affect the specified character, who also gains +2 die bonus on any Social rolls against him. If the specified character is attempting Social maneuvering on the Swooning character, the impression level is considered one higher. <br /> <b>Possible Sources</b>: Be on the receiving end of an exceptional success of a Persuasion or Subterfuge roll, dramatic failure on using the Majesty Discipline, fed on non-violently by a vampire, have another character help you fulfill your Vice (if mortal). <br /> <b>Resolution</b>: Your character does something for his love interest that puts him in danger, or he opts to fail a roll to resist a Social action by the specified character.<br /> <b>Beat</b>: n/a</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I came with the idea of this condition:<br /> <br /> <b>Dream Seduced</b><br /> A character Dream Seduced wakes up with an urge in his mind, commit a sin. Not any sin, but the sin that a strange creature showed him in his dreams. He suffers a -2 die penalty to any roll that would not be directly linked to the shameful activities presented in his dreams.<br /> <b>Possible Sources</b>: Having an Incubus or Succubus invading your dream.<br /> <b>Resolution</b>: Your character manage to keep control of his emotions for a week, or he opts to fail and give in to the shameful activities the incubus/succubus gave him in his dream.<br /> <b>Beat</b>: If your character give up and let the seducer have the best of him, this condition ends and he gains a Beat.<br /> <br /> I don't know if you find this appropriated, but that's the best I can think in this moment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Homebrews:</span></b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1492909-horodrigo-homebrews-2ed\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Vampire Homebrews</b></span></a><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000CD\"><b>Mage Legacies:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1074696-2ed-legacy-experiment-infernal-ones\" target=\"_blank\">Infernal Ones</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1070254-2e-legacies-conversions-and-homebrew?p=1078103#post1078103\" target=\"_blank\">Daoine</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1536102-homebrew-legacy-the-changed\" target=\"_blank\">Changed</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7210-horodrigo","name":"Horodrigo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7210_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-23T13:39:15-07:00","id":637341,"title":"","content":"The Obsession Condition could also work, though it both punishes actions not related to the obsession and benefits actions related to it (rather than just the former).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Something's on your character's mind and they just can't shake it. They gain the 9-again quality on all rolls related to pursuing their obsession. On rolls that are unrelated to their obsession, they lose the 10-again quality. Obsession can be a temporary quality per Storyteller approval.<br /> <br /> Beat: Character fails to fulfill an obligation due to pursuing her obsession.<br /> <br /> Resolution: The character sheds or purges her fixation. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T02:03:36-07:00","id":638881,"title":"","content":"Both are interesting conditions... The Dream Seduction one actually works perfectly with one of the characters. The Obsession one might be a tad too disruptive, although it makes sense for the other character. I will have a chat with the involved players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4560-madfox11","name":"Madfox11","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}